00:00:02.688 --> 00:00:05.390
ALLAN NAIM: So by way of
intro, I'm Allan Naim.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.380
I'm on the product
management team at Google.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.680
I work on Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.720
And these days,
I've been spending

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.360
most of my time working on
how to bring Google Kubernetes

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.055
Engine outside of Google
Cloud primarily on premise.

00:00:20.055 --> 00:00:21.650
GRACE MOLLISON: Hi I'm Grace.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.420
I'm a cloud solutions architect.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.480
And as part of my role, I get
to talk to lots of customers.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.900
And I am looking for
repeating patterns

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.689
that I can then figure
out how to share

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.080
those learnings [? via ?]
solutions and tutorials.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:48.190
So Allan, I'm here to
actually talk to you about why

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:50.740
customers move to the cloud.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.010
So I'm going to talk about
a subset of our customers,

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:57.050
financial services customers.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.840
So why are they
moving to the cloud?

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.200
It's not because it's a fact.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.140
It's because they've
got some solid reasons,

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.000
as you can see as to why
they'd want to do this.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.165
So one of the key ones fresh out
the door tends to be agility.

00:01:09.165 --> 00:01:11.540
I'll sit down and I start
saying I want to be more agile.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:14.110
They want their developers
to be able to experiment

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.530
without them having to kind
of invest in upfront Capex.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.960
They're also in a lot
of cases, their network

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.020
isn't actually designed
for global collaboration

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.838
and global delivery.

00:01:24.838 --> 00:01:26.380
So obviously, they
think of the cloud

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.463
will help them to do that.

00:01:27.463 --> 00:01:29.470
And obviously with
the scale of GCP,

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.080
that will obviously help them.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.210
And then they've
got, over the time,

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:37.490
they've had lots of problems
with single points failure,

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:39.730
because the way
things have developed.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:42.250
So they're thinking about
how they can actually

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:43.720
provide better availability.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.470
And because when you
design for the cloud,

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:47.110
we actually kind
of encourage you

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.150
to think about failures
up front, because things

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.390
fail all the time.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:56.340
And then there's the cost
optimization, which they also

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.030
are kind of key.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:02.030
So some of the workloads
this subset think about

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.000
are risk and
regulated reporting.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.490
They've got more and
more data and rules

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:08.990
that they need to comply with.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.240
So they want to do lots of
analytics and big data type

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.782
crunching, so things they need
to prove that they're actually

00:02:13.782 --> 00:02:15.020
doing as they say.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.140
Then there's financial
risk modeling, lots of CPU.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.850
And they're also
always developing

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:21.477
new consumer applications.

00:02:21.477 --> 00:02:23.060
ALLAN NAIM: So Grace,
you're out there

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.820
talking to a lot of customers
and having conversations

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:26.978
around their cloud journey.

00:02:26.978 --> 00:02:29.520
What are some of the challenges
that you're seeing out there?

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.603
GRACE MOLLISON: I knew you
were going to ask that.

00:02:31.603 --> 00:02:34.670
So as been financial services,
one of the first things

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.410
out the door is regulations.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.150
They're global.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.525
So not only have
they got to deal

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:42.140
with regulations
for local regions,

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.930
but also global regulations.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.420
And as well as
just regulations, I

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:51.170
speak to all the teams
that have an influence

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.620
and actually use the platforms.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.578
So let's have a look at
what the infrastructure

00:02:54.578 --> 00:02:56.540
teams tend to require.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:01.010
They have locality concerns
as well, so two types.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:01.760
It's the workload.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.370
Some of the workloads
can be run on premises.

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.870
Some can be run on the cloud.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.617
In addition to that, they've
got where can your data be?

00:03:09.617 --> 00:03:11.450
They want to be able
to classify their data,

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:15.130
because they just cannot
move that data to the cloud.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.760
And even when it
gets to the cloud,

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.900
it's like, oh, hang on a minute.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.600
Where in the cloud can it be?

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.090
So they won't actually
pin it to a region.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.730
And when that data
moves up, they also

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:28.160
want to be able to identify it.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.590
They're also very, very
concerned about the fact

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:32.900
that they've got to
run their operations

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.450
across a hybrid platform.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:39.920
So they're worried about the
technical complexity of that.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.840
So I spoke earlier about the
single points of failure.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.130
So it's all about
business continuity.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.320
So the fact that they
want their infrastructure

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.930
and their application interfaces
to be designed and built

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.730
for resiliency.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.130
And also they want to
make sure that their calls

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:59.450
to those interfaces are
authenticate and authorized.

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:01.580
They want to have
logical separation.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:04.370
They want to be able to
distinguish between workloads

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.300
and team projects.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.540
And then they want guardrails.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.300
Basically, they want to
be able to propagate rules

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.630
from the organization
downwards without getting

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.130
in the way of the developers.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:19.700
So they want to mitigate
against potential human error.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.550
They want to actually
stop ne'er do wells

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.397
having unauthorized access.

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:26.730
And they want to be able to
get signals for all of this.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.610
They want to be able to monitor
that hybrid architecture,

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.450
whether it be on platform
or on prem or in the cloud.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.890
And there's consistency of the
infrastructure and the policies

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:41.750
that apply across
both platforms.

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:46.950
The security team have also
got very similar requirements

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:47.530
as well.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:49.980
They also want to be
able to classify data.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:54.160
And they have issues around
where certain data should be.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.860
They want to be able to
integrate their security

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.620
controls as part of their
pipeline deployment,

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.480
be that whether it's
infrastructure's code

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:05.020
to actually deploy to the
actual infrastructure,

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:06.500
or whether it's
application codes.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.260
They want those controls
to be as part of those

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.910
that deployment and they want
it to be a first class citizen.

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.770
Data integrity-- they
want to make sure

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:16.985
that secure access to data.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:21.770
One thing there's a
common ask is like, OK,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:23.390
once we've got our
data in the cloud,

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:27.640
can we prevent it be modified
for a set period of time?

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:31.480
Auditing-- again, here
the actual act of auditing

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.960
shouldn't change anything,
forensic auditing.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.210
And they obviously been able
to track who's doing what

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.480
in their platform is key.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.890
Separation of duty,
least privilege

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.630
is actually two sides
of the same coin.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.290
It's all about permissions.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:47.560
So if you think about
the fact that you

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.870
have a central security
or network team,

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:53.080
that infers you don't want
other teams to have the ability

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.823
to change your firewall rules
or other security controls.

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.990
So it's making sure people
have the permissions they

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:02.350
need to do their job, and
not over permissioning.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:03.820
Organization-wide
policies-- that's

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:06.490
essentially the guardrails
the infrastructure team

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:08.440
talked about.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.660
And course, there's developers.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:16.600
So developers, as we said,
they want to be more agile,

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:20.080
we want to improve the
speed of deployment.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:21.910
And one of the things
that's come up about,

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:25.060
this is the fact that people
start to adopt microservices,

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:26.680
because it allows
you to work on bits

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.550
of your application with
you to different teams.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.860
And they don't get
in each other's ways

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.440
because they can actually
independently update

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.650
that service, they can
independently scale.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:40.330
And Kubernetes has
been seen as a way

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:42.860
to do that because you
can run it on prem,

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.690
you can run in another
cloud, you can run it on us.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:48.250
Separation of
duty-- again, that's

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:50.140
all about who can do what.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:53.110
And here, they're really
concerned about separating out

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.300
the operational requirements
from the development

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.000
requirements.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Logical separation-- here, it's
isolation between environments.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.610
And that can mean between
different services

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:06.970
or it can mean between
dev, prod and QA.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:09.460
And then there's isolation
between those workloads

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.940
and services.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.790
Developers, they
love CI/CD tooling.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:17.040
So one of the things that
they wanted to actually have

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:20.730
the same tooling deployed to
whichever platform, be that one

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.580
premise or on the cloud.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.010
And they want consistency.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:26.490
They want to be able to
have one way of signaling

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:30.593
through auditing, reformation
logging, and alerting.

00:07:30.593 --> 00:07:32.010
And we're hoping
that you're going

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.645
to be able to actually help
us unblock some of these.

00:07:34.645 --> 00:07:38.340
ALLAN NAIM: These are
pretty big shoes to fill.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.330
So what better way
