00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:12.100
So we are a digital
bank, which means

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.960
we have no branches,
absolutely no branches.

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:16.960
The only branch
we have right now

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:22.140
is one branch, which is in
Shelley Street in Sydney.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:23.700
That's where our head office is.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:28.520
About four and a
half, five years ago,

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:29.840
we started a journey.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.140
And when we started our
journey, our objective

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:37.470
was to create highly consumable
interactions for our customers.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:38.220
How do we do that?

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.720
How do we take
complex data and make

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.260
them more consumable and more
meaningful for our customers?

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:43.100
That was the idea.

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:44.263
There's an example here.

00:09:44.263 --> 00:09:45.680
This is my account
actually, where

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:48.470
you could go and say using
natural language search

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:50.360
capabilities, you
could see how much

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.340
did I spend in golf
around [? Marunga. ?] I

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:53.660
play a lot of golf.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:57.920
And you can see that we
have proximity-based search.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.410
And if I have a bad day, I
end up in [? Ton ?] [? Lee, ?]

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:01.530
which is on your left.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:02.910
There's a red dot there.

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:08.390
That's where I go and kind
of try and improve my swing.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:12.860
On the left side, we've
got an example of insights,

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:14.090
oversights, and foresights.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:16.880
About four years ago we
didn't have a mobile presence.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:20.120
Today we do, and it's
a leading experience

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:24.050
at the moment in Australia.

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.970
I mentioned, we
are a digital bank.

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:29.540
And we are also providing wide
level solutions for clients.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.240
We have over around
50 different channels

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.310
that we support
today that tells you

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:36.440
the complexity, the
level of complexity

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:41.340
that we have to face in
managing our entire ecosystem.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.870
Wind back to about
five years ago.

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:44.820
This is where we were.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:47.040
I'm sure all of you
can relate to this--

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.890
active passive infrastructure.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.800
And it would take us
about six to eight hours

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.660
to deploy a beautiful
infrastructure.

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:59.280
You can see that there are lots
of monolithic applications.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:00.720
It took us a while.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.450
And then we said, you know, if
we are to be a digital bank,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.880
there's no way we could
survive in a world

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:07.585
where everything is
changing constantly.

00:11:07.585 --> 00:11:09.960
So we are all living in the
fourth Industrial Revolution,

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.210
where we know that
it's all about the fast

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:14.700
that beats the slow.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.620
And connectivity is new royalty.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.630
So how do you ship
software in minutes?

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.610
That's really, really important.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.580
As an organization,
we need to find a way

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.580
to help the organization
move faster.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:30.360
And your software
has to be shippable.

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:31.720
How do we do that?

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:33.120
This is how we've done this.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.890
This is the evolution
that we've been on.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:39.090
From the right from
their left side,

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:41.790
you will see we started with
monolithic applications, moved

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.560
on to microservices.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.873
After that, we
moved all our data

00:11:45.873 --> 00:11:47.790
to a data platform, which
I'll explain in more

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:49.590
detail in the next slide.

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:51.030
Experimentation is important.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:53.410
In order to be
really innovative,

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:58.980
you've got to be able to
learn and innovate fast.

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.417
Hence, we'll talk about that
in terms of architecture.

00:12:01.417 --> 00:12:02.500
How do we align with that?

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:05.490
How do we complement what
the business wants to do?

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:06.540
Cloud was important.

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:08.610
Moving to the cloud
was fundamental for us.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.220
And of course, with
all of this, we

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:15.840
were the first bank in
Australia to publish open APIs.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:18.545
We did this last
year, early last year.

00:12:18.545 --> 00:12:20.920
That's the pace of innovation
that we have at the moment.

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:25.080
So we went from having the
ability to sequentially

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.180
release, which is
maybe once in a month

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.400
or once in three
months, to something

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.640
like 60 to 100 releases a day.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.520
And we do this
actively in production.

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:37.560
I'll talk a little
more about that.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:41.300
Our UIs would take roughly
30 seconds to deploy.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:44.200
And I can tell you
about that as well.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:47.400
This is our current
state architecture today.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.950
From the bottom, we've
got a system of record

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:53.890
where we're publishing all
the events to a layer above.

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:55.860
And then we that's where
our enterprise data is

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:57.180
deep down in the enterprise.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:01.590
About that, we have
internet enterprise data,

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:06.130
which is stored in a data store
layer or running on Kubernetes.

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:12.150
This is where we have Cassandra,
Sparks, Solar, Elasticsearch,

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:18.360
Kafka, in memory data stores,
Spark for in-memory processing.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:20.430
Data has no end and no
beginning right now.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.190
So it cannot fit in memory.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:25.740
So we've got to find a way
to process this continuously.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:27.630
We have Redis as well.

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:29.790
And we consciously
made a decision

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:36.720
to run all our data services
in a separate cluster.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.320
And the reason is very simple.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:41.400
You have different challenges
when you think about networks.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.830
You have Node IPs that
need to be exposed.

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:45.730
You have to think about
backup strategies.

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:47.978
How do you do that
at scale using arc?

00:13:47.978 --> 00:13:49.770
You've got terabytes
and terabytes of data.

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:51.207
How do you do that?

00:13:51.207 --> 00:13:52.540
You've got to think differently.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.510
In fact, we started a
journey three years ago.

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:58.770
So we've been investing upstream
with various technologies

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:01.290
and strategies through
partners to enable

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.150
us to do this at scale.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:06.960
We also have graph databases
that we use for customer 360,

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.450
and permissions and
entitlements that we have.

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:14.730
Above that layer, you've got the
digital services, our stateless

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:15.690
applications.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:17.580
We've separated the concerns.

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:20.880
We're constantly rebuilding
our applications from source.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:24.150
Our platforms are torn
down every 60 days.

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:25.583
We have to roll it over.

00:14:25.583 --> 00:14:27.750
And with that, we have to
roll all the traffic over.

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:30.060
So imagine if you're
supporting more

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:31.900
than 50 different channels.

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:36.120
It's a highly complex
exercise to have all this done

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:37.750
in a unified manner.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:40.198
So we have the API Management
Gateway, which is Apigee.

00:14:40.198 --> 00:14:42.240
We've been partnering with
Google for a long time

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.940
now, almost four
years with Apigee.

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:47.700
And the data, the
traffic shaping

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:49.740
is done from the
Apigee layer on top.

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:53.010
And above that, we've got
open endpoint independence,

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:57.732
which means we believe
in co-creating value

00:14:57.732 --> 00:14:58.440
in the ecosystem.

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:01.260
That's the reason why
we published open APIs.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:06.330
And we have a single strategy
for all our consumers.

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:10.250
It's Open ID Connect
with OAuth too.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:12.950
And you can see that we have
other consumers as well.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:16.170
We have Salesforce, which is--

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:20.310
our internal staff is using
that for customer support.

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.620
New partners-- we also
have our partners building

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.930
their own experiences with APIs.

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:29.430
And we have to do this at
scale with all our services

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:32.750
changing continuously
multiple times a day.

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:33.790
How do we do that?

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:35.830
So we have fairly--

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:39.580
an outside in based architecture
approach with everything we do.

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:42.280
We think about customers
first and then start

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:43.630
building a solutions.

00:15:43.630 --> 00:15:46.067
Now because we have
this architecture, which

00:15:46.067 --> 00:15:47.650
is on the cloud
running on Kubernetes,

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:50.230
we can spin up
multiple environments.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:53.060
Every developer can have
an end to end environment.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:56.020
And you can see there,
we snap all your data

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:57.760
that's been published
and all the events

00:15:57.760 --> 00:16:00.160
that are coming through
almost real time.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:01.430
We split that up.

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:04.630
And we can create an end to end
environment saying, hey, only

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:08.890
are digital engineers
can access this area.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:13.650
So we control the blast radius
of the access in that area.

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:15.553
And it's all live in production.

00:16:15.553 --> 00:16:17.470
So it's like saying I've
got a mini production

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:18.303
environment running.

00:16:18.303 --> 00:16:20.257
I can have a few of
these running depending

00:16:20.257 --> 00:16:22.090
on the features that
we are trying to build,

00:16:22.090 --> 00:16:24.670
because this is all
about accelerating

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:28.210
our path to production.

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:31.740
Now I believe Netflix published
some kind of a paper on this.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:37.060
And they call this forklifting,
if I'm not mistaken.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:39.850
Then once you're happy with
that, we move over to Beta.

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:44.130
Beta is where we constantly
working with our partners.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:47.350
We are working with
our internal staff.

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:50.140
And of course, we
invite external clients

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:55.880
to also come and comment and
give us feedback continuously.

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:57.380
Lastly, of course,
once that's done,

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:58.463
we can move to production.

00:16:58.463 --> 00:16:59.930
This is really important.

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:00.977
This is a strategy.

00:17:00.977 --> 00:17:03.310
That's the secret sauce that
makes us successful when we

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:06.730
have to innovate really fast.

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:09.220
Coming to how we did
this, I'll quickly

00:17:09.220 --> 00:17:12.940
tell you about how
we've implemented this

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:15.468
and how we industrialized
Kubernetes.

00:17:15.468 --> 00:17:17.260
So we've got Kubernetes
running, of course.

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.390
And when you have
to think in terms

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:22.180
of where do you draw the
line between what a platform

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:25.750
team should be doing and
what the product team should

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:27.069
be doing?

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:28.665
So of course, you
have Kubernetes.

00:17:28.665 --> 00:17:30.040
If you had just
one or two teams,

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:33.220
you wouldn't have to worry too
much about shipping software

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:34.090
out.

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:37.015
But if you have, let's say,
about 40 teams like we do,

00:17:37.015 --> 00:17:38.890
and over hundreds and
hundreds of developers,

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:40.807
you have to think
differently about how do you

00:17:40.807 --> 00:17:42.700
industrialize this.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:45.220
We are a highly
regulated environment.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:46.420
We are regulated.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:48.990
We have obligations
for regulators.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:50.740
Hence we have to make
sure that everything

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.350
is traced and audited.

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:55.300
So we use GitOps.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:58.380
All our governance related
parameters and metadata

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:00.250
are stored in Git.

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:02.770
And if you guys are
not aware of GitOps,

00:18:02.770 --> 00:18:05.110
have a search on the
internet and you'll

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:06.380
learn a lot more about it.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:08.020
It's very interesting topic.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:12.790
Essentially, we can store
all our metadata in Git.

00:18:12.790 --> 00:18:15.550
And you can have
approvals and everything

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:18.650
done using Git itself.

00:18:18.650 --> 00:18:22.030
So this is where you have
permissions based on, hey,

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:23.980
this is the structure
of the team.

00:18:23.980 --> 00:18:27.840
Within this team, this
is the release manager.

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:29.340
This is the approver.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:31.865
This is how you get
stuff into production.

00:18:31.865 --> 00:18:33.240
If you are a
release manager, you

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:36.220
can only get stuff into
production using the JIRA.

00:18:36.220 --> 00:18:38.403
You can't go and deploy
it from anywhere else.

00:18:38.403 --> 00:18:40.320
You have to have security
boundaries drawn out

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:41.470
pretty clearly.

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:43.980
And this is why you have
to look at guardrails

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:45.063
that are really important.

00:18:45.063 --> 00:18:45.980
It could be detective.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:47.190
It could be preventive.

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:48.880
You have to think about that.

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:51.840
Another thing that really,
really challenged us

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:54.030
when we went down
the Kubernetes route

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:58.350
was you end up
with 15 x density.

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:03.630
Everybody is deploying software
in development, in test.

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:05.588
And you are running over
capacity all the time.

00:19:05.588 --> 00:19:07.088
So you have to
manage your capacity.

00:19:07.088 --> 00:19:09.210
You have to manage the
[? expiry ?] of these named

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:10.050
spaces.

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:11.760
And that itself is a challenge.

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:15.390
We have use access reviews,
which again, being regulated,

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:18.770
we have to produce all of that.

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:23.360
Now moving further up, you think
in terms of secret management,

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:25.250
at the moment, we
have a strategy

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:27.920
for managing all our
secrets and certificates.

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:29.210
That's home grown.

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:32.180
And we're now moving
towards HashiCorp world.

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:35.407
If you had 50 ways
of doing this,

00:19:35.407 --> 00:19:37.240
you would not be able
to industrialize this.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:38.900
It would be a nightmare.

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:41.620
So what we do is we have a
standard pattern in place.

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:44.600
We have standard APIs in place
that delivers this for us.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:48.410
So for us to shift over to
the vault would be really,

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:50.197
really very simple.

00:19:50.197 --> 00:19:51.780
Another good example
here would be how

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:53.760
do you handle vulnerabilities?

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:56.790
We practice SEG DevOps.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:59.250
Our security team are
building all the APIs

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:01.230
on top of all the scanners.

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:03.420
And they handle all
the false positives.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:05.250
As you all know,
it's a nightmare.

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:08.783
Think in terms of how you
manage false positives.

00:20:08.783 --> 00:20:10.200
It's almost
impossible to do that.

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:11.790
So we do that ourselves.

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:15.270
And our entire ecosystem--
we built our entire ecosystem

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:17.010
on top of Kubernetes
to enable us

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:20.060
to do this at speed and scale.

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:21.650
As you move up--

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:24.080
as I move up, this is what
we're trying to say here.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:27.830
We tried to absorb all
the responsibilities

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:31.970
from the developers,
all the platform-related

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:32.720
responsibilities.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:35.240
Anything that we're repeating,
if we are doing anything

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:38.730
once or twice, if
it's repeatable,

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:41.840
we try and absorb that
into the platform.

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:46.530
About 98% of the patterns
that we have are all reusable.

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:49.370
So here you see the lines
going further and further up.

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:51.140
And we want to move
the line of innovation

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:53.510
closer and closer
to the developers.

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:57.600
So they only have one
job and one job alone.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:01.010
And that is to create
world class experiences

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:03.052
for our customers.

00:21:03.052 --> 00:21:04.760
And the last thing
I'd like to say there,

00:21:04.760 --> 00:21:08.090
is when you think in
terms of scale here,

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:11.050
and we are constantly moving
our clusters, recreating them,

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:14.520
you got to bleed the traffic
over to the latest services.

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:16.100
So what we do is match.

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:20.390
Our platform allows us to match
clusters, match name spaces,

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:23.960
match services within
clusters using synthetics.

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:27.500
And then once we're ready,
we flick the traffics across.

00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:29.870
So this is happening
continuously during the day.

00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:32.300
In fact, we do not do
any releases [? on it. ?]

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:34.160
We do it live.

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:35.810
Everything we do is live.

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:38.820
As a bank, it's quite
challenging to get there.

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:42.140
But we've got there because
we are on the cloud today.

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:44.870
So the next thing
I'd like to say here

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:47.720
is this is an example,
a quick example of how

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:50.090
we route our traffic.

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:52.010
We have, let's say,
white label partners

00:21:52.010 --> 00:21:57.680
building their own solutions
using version 1.5, version 1.8

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:01.160
of all our services and APIs.

00:22:01.160 --> 00:22:03.910
You have Macquarie
Bank pointing to 1.7.

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:05.480
And let's say you had a bug.

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:07.427
You would say I
want to create 1.8.

00:22:07.427 --> 00:22:08.510
You going to drop that in.

00:22:08.510 --> 00:22:10.320
It'll take you
about five minutes.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:15.420
What we do is build products, a
namespace related to a product.

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:19.090
So let's say you got
personal banking namespace.

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:22.202
You'd have a wealth namespace,
an identity namespace,

00:22:22.202 --> 00:22:23.660
a business
[? mounting ?] namespace

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:24.900
and so on and so forth.

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:29.460
And you'll find most of
the time your applications,

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:32.910
your architecture, your
topology of your namespaces

00:22:32.910 --> 00:22:35.330
will always, always
reflect the way

00:22:35.330 --> 00:22:37.730
you build your software
in the organization.

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:38.820
And it's a fact.

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:40.410
You will be doing that.

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:42.810
And if you do that,
it's a challenge.

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:45.150
But we'll come to that
challenge as well.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:48.230
Now, what we do here
is also you could just

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:51.020
drop in or change any
microservice here.

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:56.270
What we prefer to do is move
them as a set or as a product

00:22:56.270 --> 00:22:59.360
through the entire lifecycle
from development right

00:22:59.360 --> 00:23:03.680
through to integration, unit
testing, and production, lab

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:04.660
and production.

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:06.800
Our new features, as
I mentioned before, we

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:09.500
have no limit to the number
of nodes you want to create.

00:23:09.500 --> 00:23:10.820
We have multiple clusters.

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:13.640
We have different clusters for
different purposes, enterprise

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:16.130
services, legacy applications.

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:20.300
We have digital services,
stateful, stateless,

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:21.420
you name it, we have it.

00:23:21.420 --> 00:23:23.087
And we have multiple
touch environments.

00:23:23.087 --> 00:23:26.840
And we have to do
this constantly.

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:31.130
So having said that, we are
now releasing to production

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:33.353
almost 60 to 100 times a day.

00:23:33.353 --> 00:23:34.520
So what does it mean for us?

00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:36.950
What's next for us?

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:38.060
And this is interesting.

00:23:38.060 --> 00:23:41.540
We have spent a lot of effort
in the past three years

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.220
managing Kubernetes.

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:44.400
It's not an easy task.

00:23:44.400 --> 00:23:45.980
It's not for the faint hearted.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:49.430
And we've also been
curating from upstream

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:51.110
and bringing it in.

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:53.800
We actually working
with partners upstream.

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:55.680
And it's a challenge for us.

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:59.090
What we want to do is outsource
all this responsibility

00:23:59.090 --> 00:24:00.980
to Google.

00:24:00.980 --> 00:24:03.713
We want to make sure that we
are focusing wholly and solely

00:24:03.713 --> 00:24:05.630
on one thing alone, which
is building business

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:09.020
value for our customers
and our partners.

00:24:09.020 --> 00:24:11.480
Now if you think about
what next for us,

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:14.870
I talked about how we are
building our software today.

00:24:14.870 --> 00:24:17.390
It's fairly siloed.

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:18.873
We have products.

00:24:18.873 --> 00:24:20.540
And let's see our
business banking world

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:21.770
and personal banking.

00:24:21.770 --> 00:24:26.440
And now you want
to start creating

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:28.510
services that can be shared.

00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:30.220
This is where the
challenge comes.

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:31.060
So you build it.

00:24:31.060 --> 00:24:33.590
You build a
namespace, and you say

00:24:33.590 --> 00:24:36.580
this is the payment gateway
for all the products.

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:38.915
And then you start
thinking in terms of, hey,

00:24:38.915 --> 00:24:40.290
I need to worry
about the service

00:24:40.290 --> 00:24:42.220
to service interactions.

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:44.560
I need to start thinking
about east-west interactions,

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:46.030
rather than north, south.

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:47.180
That's what we do a lot.

00:24:47.180 --> 00:24:50.530
We have a lot of API.

00:24:50.530 --> 00:24:53.590
We use a lot of the API
gateway with the authentication

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:54.670
and authorization.

00:24:54.670 --> 00:24:56.860
What also happens in our
current state architecture

00:24:56.860 --> 00:25:00.700
is we happen to have
all sorts of patterns

00:25:00.700 --> 00:25:02.560
of authentication
and authorization

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:04.510
spanned across all our services.

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:06.800
We have defense by
depth, of course.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:11.150
But just to manage that
is a huge overhead.

00:25:11.150 --> 00:25:12.340
A lot of automation goes in.

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:13.340
A lot of energy goes in.

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:17.030
We expend a lot of effort in
trying to do this efficiently.

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:20.860
So we would love to
have one control plane.

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:23.690
We'd love to simplify
that entire architectures

00:25:23.690 --> 00:25:27.640
so we can enable our
teams to morph into more

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:28.750
feature-based development.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:32.530
We want the flexibility
to move faster and faster.

00:25:32.530 --> 00:25:35.390
And we believe that that's
slowing us down at the moment.

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:39.940
So we are excited to
be working with Google.

00:25:39.940 --> 00:25:43.220
And what's it called again?

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:44.260
It's Anthos.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:45.910
I'm still getting used to it.

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:47.300
I just got announced today.

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:49.600
So I remember it as CSP.

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:51.820
So excited to work
with Google on this.

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:56.020
We are hoping that you
continue to curate technology

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:58.320
from upstream, bring it to
us, make it end price great,

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:00.010
and any price
radian we can focus

00:26:00.010 --> 00:26:03.280
on what we do best, which
is building the business

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:04.570
value for our customers.

00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:10.030
Lastly, I like to say
that we at Macquarie,

00:26:10.030 --> 00:26:12.730
continue to aspire to dream
in months, plan in days,

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:14.030
and ship in minutes.

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:15.640
I'm around here
after this meeting.

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:18.610
If there's any other questions,
I'm happy to take them.

00:26:18.610 --> 00:26:19.682
Thank you very much.

00:26:19.682 --> 00:26:20.506
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:20.506 --> 00:26:21.760
ALLAN NAIM: Thank you Rajay.

00:26:21.760 --> 00:26:24.706
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:32.050
So we designed Anthos
really to cater

00:26:32.050 --> 00:26:36.370
to the needs of enterprise
customers like Macquarie.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:38.620
So the example that
Rajay walked through

00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:41.080
is very much the area
that we're looking

00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:45.280
at addressing with Anthos
so that as customers, you

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:47.830
can outsource a lot of the
complexities associated

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:50.380
with managing these
environments and being

00:26:50.380 --> 00:26:54.190
able to implement centralized
governance across environments

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:55.990
that are very
heterogeneous in nature,

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:59.080
but do it in a way that's
predictable and consistent.

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:00.830
That's really what
Anthos is about.

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:03.010
So today we announced Anthos.

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:05.920
It's the latest
innovation from Google.

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:09.220
It's around hybrid and
multi-cloud computing.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:12.130
It's for modernizing
applications, automating

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:16.270
policy, and it provides
you security at scale.

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:21.910
And it'll run on prem, in
Google Cloud, and other clouds.

00:27:21.910 --> 00:27:25.750
So part of the challenge with
hybrid traditionally has been--

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:27.850
hybrid is complex.

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:30.160
When you start thinking
about deploying applications

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:32.350
across multiple
environments, you

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:36.220
start addressing challenges
around operational overhead

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:39.160
and cost, because you're having
to use many different tools

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:41.950
and bring in people of
different skill sets.

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:44.090
And it's not just
about the technology.

00:27:44.090 --> 00:27:45.850
It's about the
people and process.

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:50.140
That's complicated in terms of
how you make all that happen.

00:27:50.140 --> 00:27:53.320
Kubernetes did a really
good job at abstracting away

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:55.000
the underlying infrastructure.

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:56.407
And we're all happy about.

00:27:56.407 --> 00:27:58.240
Just show of hands, how
many people out here

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:00.760
are using Kubernetes?

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:02.320
That's awesome.

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:04.270
And Kubernetes has done
a very good job there.

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:07.120
But as you start building
more and more containerized

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:10.480
applications and exposing
services, and services

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:14.740
that live on prem, in the
cloud, on other clouds,

00:28:14.740 --> 00:28:16.750
how do you manage all
these interactions?

00:28:16.750 --> 00:28:20.150
How do you implement
a preventive policy

00:28:20.150 --> 00:28:23.020
across who can do what and
what applications are allowed

00:28:23.020 --> 00:28:24.970
to do cross this environment?

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:27.140
These are really
hard things to do.

00:28:27.140 --> 00:28:29.140
And then getting
started with Kubernetes,

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:31.990
typically, cloud providers
do a very good job

00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:34.960
at providing managed
Kubernetes implementations.

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:39.130
But what if you have to get
started outside of cloud?

00:28:39.130 --> 00:28:43.900
There's 10, 20, 30
distributions out there.

00:28:43.900 --> 00:28:45.130
Each is different.

00:28:45.130 --> 00:28:48.730
But what if you had an ability
to get a Google supported

00:28:48.730 --> 00:28:51.910
Google distribution
of Kubernetes, which

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:55.420
is Google Kubernetes Engine,
and pretty much run it anywhere

00:28:55.420 --> 00:28:57.040
starting with on prem?

00:28:57.040 --> 00:28:58.750
And that's what we've
done with Anthos.

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:02.290
We provided a GKE
on prem distribution

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:04.330
that you can run in
your data centers that's

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:07.630
fully consistent with
GKE in the cloud,

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:09.280
so that you can
build applications

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:13.880
for one environment and be
able to run them anywhere.

00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:16.670
And then let's not forget
about operations, the day

00:29:16.670 --> 00:29:19.280
two experience,
the ability to be

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:23.030
able to define central policy,
and apply these policies

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:25.850
across all of your
cluster environments

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:27.560
in a consistent fashion.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:29.870
Rajay mentioned the
whole notion of GitOps.

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:31.670
This is our goal with Anthos.

00:29:31.670 --> 00:29:33.920
It's really to drive
the GitOps model

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:35.545
so you can check your
policy and config

00:29:35.545 --> 00:29:39.100
and to Git, and have them
applied deterministicly

00:29:39.100 --> 00:29:41.030
across your cluster
environments,

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:43.100
regardless of where
they're running.

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:47.810
And then we're providing
a centralized monitoring

00:29:47.810 --> 00:29:52.310
and logging framework via
Stackdriver and Prometheus

00:29:52.310 --> 00:29:56.060
for your entire operations for
your hybrid environments as

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:58.500
well.

00:29:58.500 --> 00:30:02.920
So let's talk about how GCP
and Anthos can help you.

00:30:02.920 --> 00:30:05.470
Grace, you start
off the presentation

00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:09.480
talking about all the challenges
that you're seeing out there.

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:11.650
So let's walk through
these challenges

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:14.680
and talk about how
we address them.

00:30:14.680 --> 00:30:18.340
So Grace started off
talking about regulation.

00:30:18.340 --> 00:30:21.550
Customers operate in a
very regulated environment.

00:30:21.550 --> 00:30:24.490
Google Cloud has achieved
independent verification

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:28.240
of security, privacy,
and compliance controls,

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:30.740
achieving certification
against global standards.

00:30:30.740 --> 00:30:33.660
So that's done.

00:30:33.660 --> 00:30:36.680
Now let's talk about
infrastructure requirements.

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:39.440
So Anthos enables
platform operators

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:41.630
to consistently run
and centrally manage

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:46.340
their Kubernetes clusters
across on prem and the cloud.

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:48.770
This includes GKE
On Prem, that I just

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:51.260
talked about, which is
a GKE distribution that

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:53.330
runs on premise.

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:56.840
On top of that, Anthos
provides centralized management

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:59.610
for your Kubernetes
clusters and services.

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:01.580
Now these services could
be container-based,

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:03.200
or you could have
a virtual machine

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:08.000
that has an envoy proxy that
now becomes part of the service

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:08.900
mesh.

00:31:08.900 --> 00:31:12.260
But you now have the
ability to centrally manage

00:31:12.260 --> 00:31:16.400
this environment across
on prem and public cloud.

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:18.230
And the important
thing is reducing

00:31:18.230 --> 00:31:20.220
the operational
overhead of operating

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:21.650
across this hybrid environment.

00:31:21.650 --> 00:31:22.620
That's key.

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:24.770
That's been one of the
biggest adoption blockers,

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:27.830
is the complexity and
the operational costs.

00:31:27.830 --> 00:31:30.770
Our aim is to reduce that.

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:33.710
For applications that have
data sensitivity and locality

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:36.950
concerns, Google Cloud
data loss prevention API

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:41.330
can be used to classify data to
ensure that it remains or gets

00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:43.590
set where it's allowable.

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:47.420
So we have sessions this
week on the DLP API.

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:49.190
Highly recommend
you check into that.

00:31:49.190 --> 00:31:54.150
And there's also a booth that
you can learn more about it.

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:56.570
Anthos has been designed
from the ground up

00:31:56.570 --> 00:31:58.730
to manage hybrid resources.

00:31:58.730 --> 00:32:02.420
And we enable you
to configure policy

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:04.760
so that you meet the
standards around your business

00:32:04.760 --> 00:32:06.170
continuity.

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:08.180
That's been very key for us.

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:13.430
Platform operators can logically
separate workloads and teams,

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:16.670
either using clusters
or namespaces.

00:32:16.670 --> 00:32:20.270
The idea here is Kubernetes,
as you start using it,

00:32:20.270 --> 00:32:22.300
you have to make
important decisions around

00:32:22.300 --> 00:32:24.690
do I create one cluster
for all my teams?

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:26.570
Do I have a cluster per team?

00:32:26.570 --> 00:32:28.790
Do I create a
namespace per team?

00:32:28.790 --> 00:32:30.490
We're providing all
the various tooling

00:32:30.490 --> 00:32:32.060
to enable you to
make the choice.

00:32:32.060 --> 00:32:33.890
You have freedom of
choice around what

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:35.310
model works for you.

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:38.060
But then define config
across all these environments

00:32:38.060 --> 00:32:40.740
in a consistent fashion.

00:32:40.740 --> 00:32:43.280
And a common unified
monitoring and logging service

00:32:43.280 --> 00:32:45.350
across your hybrid
environments really that

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:49.630
minimizes the risk
with exposing services.

00:32:49.630 --> 00:32:52.830
Now let's talk about the
security team requirements.

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:55.320
So Anthos allows you
to run your workloads

00:32:55.320 --> 00:32:57.750
and have your data
reside where you need it.

00:32:57.750 --> 00:33:01.410
And again, this is where
DLP comes in, where you can

00:33:01.410 --> 00:33:04.530
classify your data ensure
that data doesn't end up where

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:06.300
in places it shouldn't.

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:08.670
And then you can
leverage existing CI/CD

00:33:08.670 --> 00:33:12.010
tooling to implement
container supply chain,

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:17.110
and implement things like image
scanning binary authorization

00:33:17.110 --> 00:33:20.410
and so forth across your
container supply chain.

00:33:20.410 --> 00:33:22.500
And you get real
time visibility.

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:26.520
Think of Istio as real time
monitoring for your services.

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:28.230
You're getting real
time visibility

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:30.450
and integrated logging
across your services

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:35.400
that are running in
this hybrid environment.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:37.920
Now the cool thing
here is really

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:40.650
around configuration management
and centralized governance.

00:33:40.650 --> 00:33:44.500
So Anthos brings this notion
of GitOps into reality.

00:33:44.500 --> 00:33:46.200
So you've heard from Rajay.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:48.090
For the last couple
of years, they've

00:33:48.090 --> 00:33:50.490
been developing this
environment really

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:52.620
that's very GitOps driven.

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:55.050
We want to bring
GitOps to the masses

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:58.260
so that you can just take
advantage of it from day one.

00:33:58.260 --> 00:34:01.890
And the idea here
is really bringing

00:34:01.890 --> 00:34:05.310
to reality this whole notion
of separation of concerns.

00:34:05.310 --> 00:34:07.710
At Google we operate
in a model where

00:34:07.710 --> 00:34:14.219
we have cluster operators,
application ops, and security

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:17.310
admins really operating
independently, but working

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:18.420
together.

00:34:18.420 --> 00:34:21.840
And we're bringing this model
of separation of concerns

00:34:21.840 --> 00:34:25.500
so that administrators can
define preventative policies

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:27.780
that can be centrally
defined and propagated

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:29.679
across a hybrid environment.

00:34:29.679 --> 00:34:31.230
That's really key for us.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:33.420
So Anthos configuration
management

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:38.250
enables cluster, security,
network admin teams

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:42.540
to operate independently and
define configuration centrally.

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:45.840
And configuration can be owned
by these independent teams.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:48.600
And it really decouples the
infrastructure and security

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:51.389
concerns away from
the development team.

00:34:51.389 --> 00:34:53.190
That's really the idea here.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:54.280
You can start small.

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:56.250
You can have every
team go and build out

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:57.820
their own environment.

00:34:57.820 --> 00:34:59.430
You can define these
policies and can

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:01.150
fix for each one of
these environments.

00:35:01.150 --> 00:35:03.630
And then you can centrally
govern them over time,

00:35:03.630 --> 00:35:06.660
so that you're not introducing
friction from the start.

00:35:09.730 --> 00:35:12.100
Developers are super
critical for us.

00:35:12.100 --> 00:35:15.310
Our goal here is to enable
developers to move fast.

00:35:15.310 --> 00:35:17.290
Really, when you
think about it, Anthos

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:20.440
is a platform for
agile development.

00:35:20.440 --> 00:35:24.130
It's not in a sense
opinionated to the point

00:35:24.130 --> 00:35:28.960
where developers can't do what
they feel comfortable in doing,

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:32.890
but it provides a framework to
enable developers to interact

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:34.960
with the rest of the
broader team in a way that's

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:36.380
frictionless.

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:38.620
And the idea is to
enable developers

00:35:38.620 --> 00:35:41.290
to be able to deploy
applications transparently

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:45.590
into these multiple environments
that look very different.

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:48.040
And then finally,
we have services

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:49.870
that we've built
at Google to really

00:35:49.870 --> 00:35:54.730
make the life of
applications, DevOps easier,

00:35:54.730 --> 00:35:58.180
things like Cloud Build
that are tightly integrated

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:00.700
with our container supply chain.

00:36:00.700 --> 00:36:02.980
We provide these
add-ons for Anthos.

00:36:02.980 --> 00:36:06.280
But we'll work with any CI/CD
tool that's there on the market

00:36:06.280 --> 00:36:09.340
to provide a great experience.

00:36:09.340 --> 00:36:12.160
And really, the goal here
is around consistency,

00:36:12.160 --> 00:36:14.410
and ensuring that
you're not spending time

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:16.930
learning different tools
for different environments.

00:36:20.945 --> 00:36:22.750
GRACE MOLLISON: So
that sounds great.

00:36:22.750 --> 00:36:26.620
So let me just summarize what
you've just kind of spoke

00:36:26.620 --> 00:36:29.140
to me about, is the fact
that you're telling me

00:36:29.140 --> 00:36:32.950
that I can actually get rid
of all the operational craft

00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:35.650
so that I can have a
single pane of glass

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:39.403
to manage both on
prem and in the cloud?

00:36:39.403 --> 00:36:40.070
ALLAN NAIM: Yes.

00:36:40.070 --> 00:36:41.710
GRACE MOLLISON:
Oh, that's so cool.

00:36:41.710 --> 00:36:43.450
And also that I
can then just focus

00:36:43.450 --> 00:36:45.270
on the business value,
which is basically

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:47.870
what I'm being paid for.

00:36:47.870 --> 00:36:50.440
And I love the idea of the
fact that I can segregate data.

00:36:50.440 --> 00:36:52.970
You've mentioned various
ways that we can do that.

00:36:52.970 --> 00:36:55.660
And it gives me a solid
foundation and room

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:59.140
to grow as Rajay
showed us last--

00:36:59.140 --> 00:37:00.230
earlier.

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:02.660
So this sounds great,
but how do I get started?

00:37:06.710 --> 00:37:08.210
ALLAN NAIM: Who
wants to see a demo?

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:11.815
Enough talk.

00:37:11.815 --> 00:37:13.190
Let's see some of
this in action.

00:37:13.190 --> 00:37:19.861
So I'm going to invite the Duke
up on stage to make this real.

00:37:19.861 --> 00:37:21.120
JAMES DUKE: Hi everyone.

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:22.520
My name is James Duke.

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:25.190
I'm in our professional
services group.

00:37:25.190 --> 00:37:27.920
Nicknamed the Duke,
like Allan says.

00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:31.932
I have the challenging
task today of showing you

00:37:31.932 --> 00:37:33.890
what this would actually
look like in practice.

00:37:33.890 --> 00:37:35.390
And so Grace has told us--

00:37:35.390 --> 00:37:37.057
thank you for that--
all of the problems

00:37:37.057 --> 00:37:38.973
that we see out there--
we've heard from Rajay

00:37:38.973 --> 00:37:40.285
how Macquarie have solved this.

00:37:40.285 --> 00:37:42.650
And we've heard from
Allan how CSP solves this.

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:45.180
Let's see what that actually
looks like in practice.

00:37:45.180 --> 00:37:47.510
So imagine the workflow.

00:37:47.510 --> 00:37:49.837
You are a financial
services customer

00:37:49.837 --> 00:37:51.920
with your business, which
I expect a number of you

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:53.390
are out here already.

00:37:53.390 --> 00:37:56.840
And you now want to roll
out some kind of new service

00:37:56.840 --> 00:37:59.720
within your business,
whatever it might be.

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:01.550
For the purposes
of this demo, I'm

00:38:01.550 --> 00:38:03.800
going to use a use
a demo, which shows

00:38:03.800 --> 00:38:06.860
a very simple application,
which is just grabbing data

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:09.980
from the Pub/Sub, the
Google Pub/Sub API.

00:38:09.980 --> 00:38:11.930
Now to actually
release that, what

00:38:11.930 --> 00:38:13.950
might our Git Ops
process look like?

00:38:13.950 --> 00:38:15.992
Well, the first thing you
would likely want to do

00:38:15.992 --> 00:38:17.960
is review and update
your security posture

00:38:17.960 --> 00:38:21.050
and your guardrails
that Rajay mentioned,

00:38:21.050 --> 00:38:23.230
in order to accommodate
that new application.

00:38:23.230 --> 00:38:26.857
So the place to do that is
going to be in a repository.

00:38:26.857 --> 00:38:28.940
In this case, I'm going
to use Google Cloud Source

00:38:28.940 --> 00:38:31.730
Repositories to show you
what that would look like.

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:34.130
You will go in there,
review, and update and submit

00:38:34.130 --> 00:38:35.030
that change.

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:37.340
At that point in time,
any of the governance

00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:38.990
process that you have would run.

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:40.490
We only have 10
minutes here, so I'm

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:43.323
going to skip the asking
my manager for approval.

00:38:43.323 --> 00:38:45.740
But in theory, you could put
any of the workflows in there

00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:47.400
you want.

00:38:47.400 --> 00:38:50.310
Then those changes would then
propagate out automatically

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:52.740
into all of the fleet and
all of the environments

00:38:52.740 --> 00:38:55.970
that are managed by Anthos.

00:38:55.970 --> 00:38:57.350
Finally, we can validate.

00:38:57.350 --> 00:38:58.530
Those changes are in place.

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:01.360
And then finally, as we've
mentioned here, profit.

00:39:01.360 --> 00:39:03.230
At the end of the
day, this is a tool

00:39:03.230 --> 00:39:05.520
to solve business problems.

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:08.750
And so in order to do that,
what I'm going to show you

00:39:08.750 --> 00:39:12.510
is the use of a custom resource
definition and accompanying

00:39:12.510 --> 00:39:13.940
controller.

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:16.930
And what that does is the CRD,
or custom resource definition,

00:39:16.930 --> 00:39:19.370
it allows me to define
something that's

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:21.520
Kubernetes does not
currently know about

00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:24.600
or that Anthos does not
currently know about.

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:28.350
The controller then allows me to
write the logic that translates

00:39:28.350 --> 00:39:31.320
that resource definition into
something that Kubernetes

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:32.040
understands.

00:39:32.040 --> 00:39:33.662
And it could be anything.

00:39:33.662 --> 00:39:35.370
For this case, what
we're going to do is,

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:38.570
we're going to grant this app
access to the outside world.

00:39:38.570 --> 00:39:41.960
And in order to do that,
we will use an Istio.

00:39:41.960 --> 00:39:43.635
So along the top
here, you see what

00:39:43.635 --> 00:39:44.760
it looks like conceptually.

00:39:44.760 --> 00:39:48.340
And in the bottom, you'll
see what it is in practice.

00:39:48.340 --> 00:39:48.980
All right.

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:51.890
If we could switch
over the view,

00:39:51.890 --> 00:39:54.160
you see here I
have a graph, which

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:57.160
is showing the traffic, the
consumption from the Pub/Sub

00:39:57.160 --> 00:39:59.210
API for this
particular application.

00:39:59.210 --> 00:40:01.550
As you can see, it's
effectively zero.

00:40:01.550 --> 00:40:03.400
The peak there is
from this morning

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:05.720
when I tested this demo.

00:40:05.720 --> 00:40:09.270
So what I'm going to do is
submit an actual change.

00:40:09.270 --> 00:40:11.615
So you see here in the
logs deadline exceeded,

00:40:11.615 --> 00:40:13.490
deadline exceeded,
because the application is

00:40:13.490 --> 00:40:15.710
unable to access the API.

00:40:15.710 --> 00:40:23.150
I am going to push this change
into my GitOps source of truth.

00:40:23.150 --> 00:40:25.717
And I'm going to add it.

00:40:25.717 --> 00:40:26.550
I'm going to commit.

00:40:29.950 --> 00:40:32.440
I'm going to call it Allow
Access, because that's

00:40:32.440 --> 00:40:34.490
exactly what it does.

00:40:34.490 --> 00:40:36.710
And then I'm going to push
it into the source repo.

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:41.460
Now what that will do is take
my custom resource definition

00:40:41.460 --> 00:40:43.930
and push it into my repository.

00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:46.620
Then Google's configuration
management tool

00:40:46.620 --> 00:40:48.180
is going to pull
that out of the repo

00:40:48.180 --> 00:40:52.080
and push it out to all of the
clusters that are in scope.

00:40:52.080 --> 00:40:58.430
If I show you the CRD itself,
we can see what it does.

00:40:58.430 --> 00:41:02.880
So it is of type application
security boundary.

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:04.760
This is a custom type
that we have defined,

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:06.860
which specifies a set
of application security

00:41:06.860 --> 00:41:08.490
boundaries.

00:41:08.490 --> 00:41:13.230
It's called Why and it applies
to the namespace deal app 1.

00:41:13.230 --> 00:41:15.390
Imagine we are in FSI company.

00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:17.140
We have many, many
different things spaces

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:18.030
doing many different things.

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:20.188
This particular namespace
is for a particular type

00:41:20.188 --> 00:41:22.230
of application, which is
doing it brokering deals

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:24.670
with third parties
from a marketplace.

00:41:24.670 --> 00:41:27.450
And all I'm going to
do is allow access

00:41:27.450 --> 00:41:31.610
to Google APIs with the
protocol Https and port 443.

00:41:31.610 --> 00:41:36.210
Then behind the scenes, config
management is pushing us out.

00:41:36.210 --> 00:41:39.540
Now if I refresh
my logs, you will

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:42.090
see now I have received
B, received Hello 1,

00:41:42.090 --> 00:41:43.290
received Hello 2.

00:41:43.290 --> 00:41:46.170
That's the traffic that is
being pushed into this Pub/Sub

00:41:46.170 --> 00:41:47.200
subscription.

00:41:47.200 --> 00:41:51.150
So now by pushing that
one CRD into my Git Repo,

00:41:51.150 --> 00:41:53.970
I have opened up just
for that namespace access

00:41:53.970 --> 00:41:55.910
to that one particular
set of APIs.

00:41:55.910 --> 00:41:57.423
Now, this is Kubernetes.

00:41:57.423 --> 00:41:58.590
So we can use the hierarchy.

00:41:58.590 --> 00:41:59.890
We can specify a namespace.

00:41:59.890 --> 00:42:00.840
We could do it global.

00:42:00.840 --> 00:42:02.640
We can be quite
specific about it.

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:05.700
We can apply it to specific
namespaces in specific security

00:42:05.700 --> 00:42:06.720
rings.

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:09.330
So imagine now that this--
well, this particular demo

00:42:09.330 --> 00:42:12.520
there is an on prem component.

00:42:12.520 --> 00:42:14.700
We've created the
system in such a way

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:16.650
as there are two security
teams, one of which

00:42:16.650 --> 00:42:18.660
encompasses on prem and
cloud, and one of which

00:42:18.660 --> 00:42:19.750
is just on prem.

00:42:19.750 --> 00:42:21.630
So if I have those
particular workloads,

00:42:21.630 --> 00:42:23.900
then I know I can't
push into the cloud.

00:42:23.900 --> 00:42:26.880
I can keep them in
those namespaces

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:29.940
which are security tiers,
which only on prem.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:33.570
Now as you can see, that
traffic is appearing.

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:37.830
And as you can see, right on
cue, the traffic is spiking.

00:42:37.830 --> 00:42:41.292
So this is a very trite
example, because all I've done

00:42:41.292 --> 00:42:43.500
is really like, for those
of you familiar with Istio,

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:45.980
what I've really done is
create a service entry.

00:42:45.980 --> 00:42:47.430
However, think about this.

00:42:47.430 --> 00:42:50.550
That CRD is not
limited to one thing.

00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:52.720
It can incorporate
almost anything.

00:42:52.720 --> 00:42:57.110
So what we are-- our goal here
is to write these templates

00:42:57.110 --> 00:42:58.110
and share them with you.

00:42:58.110 --> 00:43:01.080
If we could switch back
to the slides, please.

00:43:01.080 --> 00:43:04.810
We will create a
situation where we

00:43:04.810 --> 00:43:10.020
produce these templates
sized CRDs which we will then

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:11.420
publish to the world.

00:43:11.420 --> 00:43:15.117
And the idea is to
solve Rajay's point.

00:43:15.117 --> 00:43:16.950
If they were going to
do this a second time,

00:43:16.950 --> 00:43:18.547
how could we do it faster?

00:43:18.547 --> 00:43:20.130
How could we lean
on Google to provide

00:43:20.130 --> 00:43:22.230
more of the best
practices out of the box

00:43:22.230 --> 00:43:24.220
that I can consume very easily?

00:43:24.220 --> 00:43:25.890
So we would produce
these templates,

00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:27.765
publish them in
GitHub to the world.

00:43:27.765 --> 00:43:29.640
And they will allow you
to very, very quickly

00:43:29.640 --> 00:43:33.600
bring these in and build on
top of them and grow them,

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:36.450
and to make your adoption--
your migration to this process

00:43:36.450 --> 00:43:38.352
much, much faster.

00:43:38.352 --> 00:43:40.185
On top of that Google
professional services,

00:43:40.185 --> 00:43:43.020
which is the team I'm in,
and our partner organizations

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:44.830
are also putting together
services packages

00:43:44.830 --> 00:43:46.680
so we can help
with this process.

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:49.880
So a combination of a
smart trained people--

00:43:49.880 --> 00:43:52.100
sounds-- I just called
myself smart there.

00:43:52.100 --> 00:43:53.585
That sound very bad.

00:43:53.585 --> 00:43:54.960
Everybody else
who's really smart

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:59.340
and me are able to deliver
these projects with you,

00:43:59.340 --> 00:44:02.723
bye leaning on some of
these templated CRDs.

00:44:02.723 --> 00:44:04.890
And our goal here is that
this answers that question

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:06.690
of what do we want from CSP?

00:44:06.690 --> 00:44:10.090
And this is what it would
look like in practice.

00:44:10.090 --> 00:44:11.062
Thank you.

